<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · West Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Nembe Kingdom</h1><p class="st-meta">1400 CE – 1884 CE (approx.) · capital Nembe</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Ijaw kingdom on the Brass river, remembered for its 1895 raid on the Royal Niger Company's base at Akassa.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Nembe was founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ijaw-people">Ijaw</a> communities in the swamps of the central-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niger-river">Niger</a> delta, traditionally by the 15th century. Its position near the Brass river mouth gave access to the sea and the Niger's channels, and Europeans called the state Brass after its outlet.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amanyanabo">Amanyanabo</a> ruled with the heads of trading houses, in a system similar to other delta states. Nembe traders bought slaves and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palm-oil">palm oil</a> from inland markets and sold them to European ships. In the 19th century Christian missions arrived, and the kingdom split into rival towns over religion and trade.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Nembe signed a British treaty in 1884 and became part of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/oil-rivers-protectorate">Oil Rivers Protectorate</a>. When the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/royal-niger-company-territories">Royal Niger Company</a> shut Nembe traders out of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niger-river">Niger</a> markets, King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-william-koko">Frederick William Koko</a> led an attack on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akassa-raid">Akassa</a> in 1895, and British forces burned Nembe in reprisal. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amanyanabo">Amanyanabo</a> still reigns.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/nembe-kingdom.jpg" alt="Nembe Kingdom" width="320" height="284"><figcaption>Image: CommonRollebon (talk) and commons · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:IjawHistory.jpeg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1400 CE – 1884 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>city state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Nembe</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Frederick William Koko</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Ijaw traditional religion and Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Nembe</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/nigeria">Nigeria</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Nembe, Brass</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1642&amp;focus=nembe-kingdom" aria-label="Nembe Kingdom on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4858 2008 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Nembe Kingdom lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5178 2264L5175 2243"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5175 2243h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5178 2264h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5178 2264h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1642&amp;focus=nembe-kingdom">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

